1 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major → P1) although HLR-4 is P2.\n4. KServe is listed as an affected component but requires no code changes for ROCm serving (ServingRuntime containers carry the amd.com/gpu requests; KServe deploys them unchanged). Classified passive; validation folded into the campaign and productization ACs (same owning team, so no separate validation epic).\n5. Priority inversion check: none — campaign (P0) gates productization (P0); training work P1 as its HLR.\n\nTiered Delivery:\n- Tier 1 (independent, start immediately): E001 campaign (uses upstream ROCm images directly), E003 training enablement, E004 quota handling. Partial value: first-hand parity data, training path support, quota readiness.\n- Tier 2 (dependency-gated): E002 productization — gated by campaign go/no-go and subject to upstream ROCm image cadence aligning with release trains.\n- E005 docs follows all implementations (Rule 11).\n\nOpen Questions Disposition:\n1. Hardware access → not an Investigation: the answer (procurement vs partner-lab) changes campaign logistics, not which epics exist or what they do. Recorded as risk on E001 (and hardware-dependent validation ACs on E003); campaign scope includes securing access.\n2. Upstream ROCm build cadence → not an Investigation: affects timing/base-image choice of productization, not its existence or content. Risk on E002 + Tier 2 gating; informational monitoring, resolution owned by AMD/upstream.\n3. The genuine unknown — functional/performance parity per model → E001 Investigation, gating E002 (rewrite on partial clear; remove if no model clears).\n\nComponent/team mapping notes:\n- All four affected components are in the canonical list and in architecture context (no onboarding chains needed).\n- E001/E002 assigned component \"Serving Runtimes\" (vLLM runtime is the validated/productized artifact; the KServe deployment surface is validated within campaign/productization ACs — same team).\n- E003 \"Training Operator\", E004 \"Distributed Workloads\", E005 \"Documentation\" (canonical).\n\nAlso mention conditional-branch decision: campaign outcomes (go / partial / no-go) are bounded but change only E002's scope, not the epic structure — modeled as a single gated epic with gate_failure_impact (rewrite) rather than branch files. Rationale documented.\n\nNow epic bodies. Must not reference sibling epic IDs. Let me draft each.\n\nE001 body:\n\n# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving parity for the supported model set\n\n## Description\nAn Investigation epic that resolves the strategy's central unknown: whether vLLM serving on AMD Instinct accelerators (ROCm) reaches functional and performance parity with the productized CUDA builds for the platform's supported model set. The campaign runs vLLM ROCm serving on physical AMD Instinct hardware, measures functional correctness and serving performance per model, and produces the go/no-go scope that determines which models are productized.\n\n## Scope\n- Secure access to dedicated physical AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or partner-lab arrangement) — campaign setup work; the access itself is externally controlled (see Risks).\n- Stand up vLLM ROCm serving on the AMD Instinct nodes using upstream-published ROCm runtime images, with the driver/ROCm stack the campaign will record.\n- Define the campaign's measurement plan: supported model set, functional test workloads, performance workload and metrics (throughput/latency), and the CUDA baseline comparison.\n- Run the campaign: per-model functional validation and performance measurement.\n- Produce the go/no-go report: per-model functional results, performance vs CUDA baseline, parity gaps (unsupported operators, driver issues, performance shortfalls), and the recommended productization scope.\n- Report actionable gaps to the owning teams / upstream communities.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A campaign report exists that covers every model in the supported model set, with per-model functional results and performance measurements against the CUDA baseline.\n- The report states a per-model go/no-go recommendation with supporting data, defining the productization scope.\n- The campaign methodology (hardware specification, driver and ROCm versions, model and image versions, test workloads) is recorded such that results are reproducible.\n- Identified parity gaps are documented with root cause where known, and actionable gaps are reported to the appropriate owning team or upstream community.\n- Serving is exercised end-to-end through the platform's serving path (InferenceService deployment of the vLLM ROCm runtime requesting AMD GPU resources) for at least the models cleared for productization consideration. (KServe passive validation.)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): the validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishing functional and performance parity data and the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks / Notes (extra section)\n- Hardware access is an availability precondition controlled outside the strategy (procurement or partner-lab cadence); it is not an acceptance criterion. If access is delayed, campaign start slips accordingly.\n- Upstream ROCm image cadence controls which runtime versions the campaign can test.\n\nWait — AC 5 mentions \"InferenceService\" — fine, that's the platform concept, not invented detail (KServe interfaces from arch context). OK. Though careful — \"decompose, don't design\": using InferenceService/ServingRuntime is grounded in architecture context. Fine.\n\nE002 body:\n\n# Productize the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models\n\n## Description\nAn Implementation epic that turns the validated ROCm serving capability into a productized runtime: a vLLM ROCm serving runtime image built in the midstream serving-runtimes repository, its build pipeline, and the ServingRuntime definition that makes it deployable for the models the validation campaign clears. Scope (which models are enabled) is set by the campaign's go/no-go outcome — this epic is gated on that resolution.\n\n## Scope\n- Add a ROCm build variant of the vLLM serving runtime image to the midstream serving runtimes repository, building on a released upstream ROCm base image, covering the models cleared by the validation campaign.\n- Extend the existing build pipeline for the repository to build and release the ROCm image variant alongside the CUDA builds.\n- Provide the ServingRuntime/ClusterServingRuntime definition that deploys the ROCm vLLM runtime requesting AMD GPU resources (amd.com/gpu) for inference.\n- Verify end-to-end serving through the platform's serving path for the cleared models on AMD Instinct nodes.\n- Ensure existing CUDA-based serving runtimes are unaffected.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The vLLM ROCm serving runtime image builds in the midstream repository and its build pipeline is green (including the Konflux pipeline for the repository).\n- A ServingRuntime definition deploys the ROCm vLLM runtime with amd.com/gpu resource requests, and InferenceServices using it serve the campaign-cleared models on AMD Instinct nodes.\n- The set of models enabled by default matches the campaign's go/no-go scope; models that did not clear are not enabled.\n- Existing CUDA-based serving runtimes and inference workloads on NVIDIA hardware show no regression from this change.\n- The ROCm base image and version pinned for the productized runtime are recorded.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P0): a supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for the models the validation campaign clears.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n- Gated on the validation campaign outcome: if only a subset of models clears, productize that subset; if no model clears, this epic is removed and parity gaps are reported upstream instead.\n- Upstream ROCm runtime image release cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities and may not align with the platform's release trains; the