e this strategy's control.\n- PyTorch ROCm image releases are upstream-controlled and may not align with platform release trains.\n\nE004 body:\n\n# Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for AMD Instinct workloads\n\n## Description\nAn Implementation epic covering the Distributed Workloads (Kueue) side of AMD GPU enablement: quota and admission handling for the amd.com/gpu resource so batch AI workloads requesting AMD Instinct accelerators are admitted, quota-accounted, and gang-scheduled like their NVIDIA counterparts.\n\n## Scope\n- Extend the queueing configuration to account for amd.com/gpu resources within the ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model.\n- Define resource flavors covering AMD GPU nodes so workloads requesting amd.com/gpu are matched and admitted correctly.\n- Validate quota enforcement for amd.com/gpu: admission honors quota limits, and all-or-nothing (waitForPodsReady) admission holds for multi-GPU workloads.\n- Verify the AMD quota path does not disrupt existing NVIDIA GPU quota handling and admission.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Workloads requesting amd.com/gpu are admitted against ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota with correct resource accounting for AMD GPUs.\n- All-or-nothing admission holds: a multi-GPU workload requesting amd.com/gpu is admitted only when its full request can be satisfied.\n- Quota exhaustion behavior is verified: workloads beyond quota remain pending and are admitted when quota is released.\n- Existing NVIDIA GPU quota handling and admitted workloads are unaffected.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-3 (P1): PyTorch training on ROCm supported ... with amd.com/gpu quota handling (quota/admission portion).\n\nE005 body:\n\n# Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support: hardware, drivers, and validated models\n\n## Description\nA docs-authoring epic producing the user-facing documentation for AMD GPU (ROCm) enablement: the supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, the validated model list, and the guidance users need to serve and train on AMD Instinct accelerators with the platform.\n\n## Scope (content outline inside Scope)\n- Supported AMD hardware: which AMD Instinct accelerators are supported, per the validation campaign results.\n- Driver prerequisites: required ROCm/driver stack versions for serving and training.\n- Validated model list: the models cleared by the validation campaign and served by the productized vLLM ROCm runtime.\n- Usage guidance for PyTorch training on ROCm: single- and multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota.\n- Limits and exclusions carried from the strategy: no other accelerator families, no AMD GPU sharing/partitioning.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Documentation states the supported AMD hardware and the driver/ROCm prerequisites for both serving and training.\n- Documentation states the validated model list, consistent with the campaign's go/no-go scope and what was productized.\n- Documentation includes the prerequisites and usage notes for PyTorch ROCm training jobs (single- and multi-GPU through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota).\n- Content is technically reviewed against the implemented capability before publication.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-4 (P2): documentation states supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, and the validated model list. Epic priority is P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Major).\n\nNow signal rationale files. One per epic: RHAISTRAT-9020-E001-ai-signals.md etc.\n\nNow, write the files. I'll use Write for bodies (no frontmatter), then frontmatter.py set. Note: Write tool requires... new files, fine.\n\nActually wait — for the summary, the prompt says write body (no frontmatter delimiters) then set frontmatter via script. The `set` command presumably creates/updates the frontmatter block. Let me write all bodies then run set commands.\n\nFor dependencies format: \"dependencies=RHAISTRAT-9020-E001,RHAISTRAT-9020-E003\" per example.\n\nE002 frontmatter:\nepic_id, title, parent_strat, component=\"Serving Ru