{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-dd34f9fa-5880-412e-9af5-d7543f56e56a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-869cb3dabfebcfcf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-c1vtmcry/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n\nStrategy: RHAISTRAT-9020, priority Major, status Refinement Done.\nTriage: full decomposition (not below-threshold — the strategy spans four\ncomponents, two owning teams, and multiple architecturally distinct work\nstreams: a hardware validation campaign, serving-runtime productization,\ntraining enablement, and documentation; not docs-only — code and\nconfiguration changes are required).\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving parity for the supported model set | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Enable PyTorch ROCm training for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Training Operator | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for AMD Instinct workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support: hardware, drivers, and validated models | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 — vLLM ROCm serving validation campaign (Investigation, P0)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 — Productize vLLM ROCm serving runtime (P0)\"]\n    E003[\"E003 — PyTorch ROCm training enablement (P1)\"]\n    E004[\"E004 — Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling (P1)\"]\n    E005[\"E005 — AMD ROCm documentation (docs-authoring, P1)\"]\n    E001 --> E002\n    E001 --> E005\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003 --> E005\n    E004 --> E005\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|-----------------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization epic ships — the answer rewrites E002's scope. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` (subset clear → productize the subset; no model clears → epic removed and gaps reported upstream). Outcomes are bounded (go / partial / no-go) but change only E002's scope, not the epic structure, so a single gated epic with gate metadata is used instead of conditional branch files. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring epic blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same — docs describe what was built, including the training support statement. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same — docs cover Kueue amd.com/gpu quota usage. |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (extension) | The validated model list documented by E005 is a direct deliverable of the campaign (Investigation), so E005 also waits on E001. |\n| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 | Rules 14/17 | The campaign is serving-scoped (vLLM serving parity); training enablement and Kueue quota work are independent of its outcome, live in different repos, and deliver partial value on their own → parallel (Tier 1). |\n| E003 ∥ E004 | Rule 13 (configuration-reference exception) | amd.com/gpu quota handling is queueing configuration, not an artifact the training work builds against. The coupling (\"jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota\") is an acceptance-criterion gate on the training epic, not a DAG edge. |\n| No konflux-onboarding epic | Rule 20 (inapplicable) | Serving Runtimes is already Konflux-onboarded (architecture context: `serving-runtimes.md`, \"Konflux onboarded: yes\"), and the ROCm image is a new image variant in an existing midstream repo. The pipeline extension is part of E002's scope with a \"build pipeline green\" AC (Rule 25), not a standalone onboarding epic. |\n| No repo-onboarding / license-validation epics | Rule 20 (inapplicable) | All affected components exist in architecture context and have midstream repos; no new component is being provisioned. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; functional/performance parity data; go/no-go scope for productization | E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | E002 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm for single-/multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota handling | E003 (job/runtime side), E004 (quota/admission side) |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation: supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, validated model list | E005 |\n\nFull HLR coverage — no orphaned requirements. HLR-3 splits across two epics\nbecause the job runtime (Training Operator) and the quota/admission layer\n(Distributed Workloads) are different components; both are owned by the\nDistributed Workloads team but rule 1 (different component → separate epics)\napplies. HLR-4's epic carries P1 (not P2) via the docs-authoring priority\nexception: strategy frontmatter priority is Major → P1.\n\n## Open Questions Disposition\n\n| Strategy open question | Disposition | Reasoning |\n|------------------------|-------------|-----------|\n| Hardware access (procurement or partner-lab for dedicated AMD Instinct nodes) | Risk on E001 (and hardware-dependent validation ACs on E003); campaign scope includes securing access | The answer changes campaign logistics, not which epics exist or what they do. Node access is an availability precondition granted by parties outside the strategy — recorded as a risk, never as an acceptance criterion. |\n| Upstream ROCm build cadence (AMD-controlled image releases vs our release trains) | Risk on E002 + Tier 2 gating; informational monitoring | The answer affects timing and base-image choice of productization, not its existence or content — the epic productizes whichever released upstream build aligns. Not resolvable by our technical work. |\n| Functional/performance parity per model (implicit, from HLR-1) | E001 Investigation, gating E002 | The genuine unknown: the answer changes what E002 does (which models are productized). |\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- All four affected components (KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator,\n  Distributed Workloads) are in the canonical component list and in\n  architecture context — no onboarding chains were generated.\n- **KServe is passive.** The strategy lists it as affected, but ROCm serving\n  requires no KServe code changes: the ServingRuntime's containers carry the\n  amd.com/gpu requests and KServe deploys them unchanged. Validation is folded\n  into ACs on E001 (end-to-end serving through the platform's serving path)\n  and E002 (InferenceServices using the ROCm runtime). KServe is owned by the\n  same team as Serving Runtimes (RHOAI Serving), so the passive-component\n  exception (separate validation epic for a different team) does not fire.\n- E001 and E002 both map to canonical component \"Serving Runtimes\" — the vLLM\n  runtime is the artifact being validated and productized; the KServe\n  deployment surface it runs under is exercised within those epics' ACs.\n- No component names were invented or combined; all values are verbatim from\n  `.context/rhai-components.txt` (including \"Documentation\" for the\n  docs-authoring epic).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from architecture-context\n  owning teams (KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training\n  Operator/Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads). The documentation\n  epic's team (RHOAI Docs) is an assumption — confirm with the strategy author.\n  Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs, not from strategy-level\n  ACs.\n- **Availability preconditions, not ACs.** Both strategy open questions\n  (hardware access, upstream image cadence) are externally controlled. They\n  are recorded as risks on the affected epics; the ACs sit on engineering\n  deliverables (the campaign report, the productized runtime, the validated\n  training path), per the acceptance-criteria rules.\n- **Docs priority exception.** E005 is P1 (strategy priority Major → P1)\n  although HLR-4 is P2; the docs-authoring epic is exempt from priority\n  inheritance.\n- **Priority inversions.** None found: the P0 campaign gates the P0\n  productization; training and quota work are P1 as their HLR dictates.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (upstream ROCm image cadence, and AMD hardware\navailability) has uncertain timing, but the strategy delivers partial value\nwithout release-aligned productization:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001 validation campaign (runs\n  directly on upstream-published ROCm images), E003 PyTorch ROCm training\n  enablement, E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling. Partial value:\n  first-hand parity data, a supported training path, and quota readiness.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E002 productization — gated by the campaign's\n  go/no-go outcome and subject to upstream ROCm image releases aligning with\n  the platform's release trains.\n- E005 (documentation) follows all implementation work per Rule 11.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","st