{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-6d1d32ab-cc4c-435d-a457-90dd34726b2e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-928a87101fc57300","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9020-c1vtmcry/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9020-E001.md","content":"# AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving parity for the supported model set\n\n## Description\n\nAn Investigation epic that resolves the strategy's central unknown: whether vLLM serving on AMD Instinct accelerators (ROCm) reaches functional and performance parity with the productized CUDA builds for the platform's supported model set. The campaign stands up vLLM ROCm serving on physical AMD Instinct hardware, measures functional correctness and serving performance per model, and produces the go/no-go report that determines which models are productized. Every downstream productization decision in this strategy is gated on this campaign's outcome.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Secure access to dedicated physical AMD Instinct nodes (procurement or a partner-lab arrangement) — campaign setup work; the access itself is externally controlled (see Risks).\n- Stand up vLLM ROCm serving on the AMD Instinct nodes using upstream-published ROCm runtime images, on a recorded driver/ROCm stack.\n- Define the campaign measurement plan: the supported model set, functional test workloads, performance workloads and metrics (throughput/latency), and the CUDA-baseline comparison methodology.\n- Run the campaign: per-model functional validation and performance measurement.\n- Produce the go/no-go report: per-model functional results, performance versus the CUDA baseline, parity gaps (unsupported operators, driver issues, performance shortfalls), and the recommended productization scope.\n- Report actionable parity gaps to the appropriate owning teams and upstream communities.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A campaign report exists that covers every model in the supported model set, with per-model functional results and per-model performance measurements against the CUDA baseline.\n- The report states a per-model go/no-go recommendation with supporting data, defining the productization scope.\n- The campaign methodology (hardware specification, driver and ROCm versions, model and image versions, test workloads) is recorded such that the results are reproducible.\n- Identified parity gaps are documented with root cause where known, and actionable gaps are reported to the appropriate owning team or upstream community.\n- Serving is exercised end-to-end through the platform's serving path (InferenceService deployment of a vLLM ROCm runtime requesting AMD GPU resources) for the models under consideration — this validates the serving stack needs no code changes for AMD GPUs.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): a validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware establishes functional and performance parity data for vLLM serving of the supported model set, producing the go/no-go scope for productization.\n\n## Risks\n\n- Hardware access is an availability precondition granted outside the strategy (procurement or partner-lab cadence); it is not an acceptance criterion. Delayed access slips the campaign start.\n- Upstream ROCm runtime image cadence is controlled by AMD and the upstream communities and constrains which runtime versions the campaign can test.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":51491,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b885a4284ba31d43","agent_id":"a494e83127aea9e39","session_id":"0cf99ff6-f962-401b-a34e-cfcb3645e2fd","uuid":"a2836b58-2c4e-40e4-8370-02901495e51a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:33:52.491Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9020"}
