{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93e92d9c8d52580c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9020\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9020 (AMD GPU / ROCm enablement for serving and training)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy: RHAISTRAT-9020, priority Major, status Refinement Done.\n13\tTriage: full decomposition (not below-threshold — the strategy spans four\n14\tcomponents, two owning teams, and multiple architecturally distinct work\n15\tstreams: a hardware validation campaign, serving-runtime productization,\n16\ttraining enablement, and documentation; not docs-only — code and\n17\tconfiguration changes are required).\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E001 | AMD Instinct validation campaign: vLLM ROCm serving parity for the supported model set | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E002 | Productize the vLLM ROCm serving runtime for campaign-cleared models | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E003 | Enable PyTorch ROCm training for single- and multi-GPU PyTorchJobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Training Operator | P1 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E004 | Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling for AMD Instinct workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9020-E005 | Document AMD GPU (ROCm) support: hardware, drivers, and validated models | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | Documentation | P1 |\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[\"E001 — vLLM ROCm serving validation campaign (Investigation, P0)\"]\n34\t    E002[\"E002 — Productize vLLM ROCm serving runtime (P0)\"]\n35\t    E003[\"E003 — PyTorch ROCm training enablement (P1)\"]\n36\t    E004[\"E004 — Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling (P1)\"]\n37\t    E005[\"E005 — AMD ROCm documentation (docs-authoring, P1)\"]\n38\t    E001 --> E002\n39\t    E001 --> E005\n40\t    E002 --> E005\n41\t    E003 --> E005\n42\t    E004 --> E005\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 (length 3).\n46\t\n47\t## DAG Justification\n48\t\n49\t| Edge / non-edge | Rule | Rationale |\n50\t|-----------------|------|-----------|\n51\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The campaign's go/no-go outcome determines which models the productization epic ships — the answer rewrites E002's scope. True gate: `gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` (subset clear → productize the subset; no model clears → epic removed and gaps reported upstream). Outcomes are bounded (go / partial / no-go) but change only E002's scope, not the epic structure, so a single gated epic with gate metadata is used instead of conditional branch files. |\n52\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring epic blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n53\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same — docs describe what was built, including the training support statement. |\n54\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same — docs cover Kueue amd.com/gpu quota usage. |\n55\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (extension) | The validated model list documented by E005 is a direct deliverable of the campaign (Investigation), so E005 also waits on E001. Gate metadata: `gated_by=E001`, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` (subset clear → document only the cleared subset; full no-go → serving-validated-model content collapses and the epic shrinks or is removed with the strategy). |\n56\t| E001 ∥ E003, E001 ∥ E004 | Rules 14/17 | The campaign is serving-scoped (vLLM serving parity); training enablement and Kueue quota work are independent of its outcome, live in different repos, and deliver partial value on their own → parallel (Tier 1). |\n57\t| E003 ∥ E004 | Rule 13 (configuration-reference exception) | amd.com/gpu quota handling is queueing configuration, not an artifact the training work builds against. The coupling (\"jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota\") is an acceptance-criterion gate on the training epic, not a DAG edge. |\n58\t| No konflux-onboarding epic | Rule 20 (inapplicable) | Serving Runtimes is already Konflux-onboarded (architecture context: `serving-runtimes.md`, \"Konflux onboarded: yes\"), and the ROCm image is a new image variant in an existing midstream repo. The pipeline extension is part of E002's scope with a \"build pipeline green\" AC (Rule 25), not a standalone onboarding epic. |\n59\t| No repo-onboarding / license-validation epics | Rule 20 (inapplicable) | All affected components exist in architecture context and have midstream repos; no new component is being provisioned. |\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n62\t\n63\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n64\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n65\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Validation campaign on physical AMD Instinct hardware; functional/performance parity data; go/no-go scope for productization | E001 |\n66\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Supported vLLM ROCm serving runtime productized for cleared models | E002 |\n67\t| HLR-3 | P1 | PyTorch training on ROCm for single-/multi-GPU jobs admitted through Kueue with amd.com/gpu quota handling | E003 (job/runtime side), E004 (quota/admission side) |\n68\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation: supported AMD hardware, driver prerequisites, validated model list | E005 |\n69\t\n70\tFull HLR coverage — no orphaned requirements. HLR-3 splits across two epics\n71\tbecause the job runtime (Training Operator) and the quota/admission layer\n72\t(Distributed Workloads) are different components; both are owned by the\n73\tDistributed Workloads team but rule 1 (different component → separate epics)\n74\tapplies. HLR-4's epic carries P1 (not P2) via the docs-authoring priority\n75\texception: strategy frontmatter priority is Major → P1.\n76\t\n77\t## Open Questions Disposition\n78\t\n79\t| Strategy open question | Disposition | Reasoning |\n80\t|------------------------|-------------|-----------|\n81\t| Hardware access (procurement or partner-lab for dedicated AMD Instinct nodes) | Risk on E001 (and hardware-dependent validation ACs on E003); campaign scope includes securing access | The answer changes campaign logistics, not which epics exist or what they do. Node access is an availability precondition granted by parties outside the strategy — recorded as a risk, never as an acceptance criterion. |\n82\t| Upstream ROCm build cadence (AMD-controlled image releases vs our release trains) | Risk on E002 + Tier 2 gating; informational monitoring | The answer affects timing and base-image choice of productization, not its existence or content — the epic productizes whichever released upstream build aligns. Not resolvable by our technical work. |\n83\t| Functional/performance parity per model (implicit, from HLR-1) | E001 Investigation, gating E002 | The genuine unknown: the answer changes what E002 does (which models are productized). |\n84\t\n85\t## Component Mapping Notes\n86\t\n87\t- All four affected components (KServe, Serving Runtimes, Training Operator,\n88\t  Distributed Workloads) are in the canonical component list and in\n89\t  architecture context — no onboarding chains were generated.\n90\t- **KServe is passive.** The strategy lists it as affected, but ROCm serving\n91\t  requires no KServe code changes: the ServingRuntime's containers carry the\n92\t  amd.com/gpu requests and KServe deploys them unchanged. Validation is folded\n93\t  into ACs on E001 (end-to-end serving through the platform's serving path)\n94\t  and E002 (InferenceServices using the ROCm runtime). KServe is owned by the\n95\t  same team as Serving Runtimes (RHOAI Serving), so the passive-component\n96\t  exception (separate validation epic for a different team) does not fire.\n97\t- E001 and E002 both map to canonical component \"Serving Runtimes\" — the vLLM\n98\t  runtime is the artifact being validated and productized; the KServe\n99\t  deployment surface it runs under is exercised within those epics' ACs.\n100\t- No component names were invented or combined; all values are verbatim from\n101\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt` (including \"Documentation\" for the\n102\t  docs-authoring epic).\n103\t\n104\t## Health Warnings\n105\t\n106\t- **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n107\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n108\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Teams were derived from architecture-context\n109\t  owning teams (KServe/Serving Runtimes → RHOAI Serving; Training\n110\t  Operator/Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads). The documentation\n111\t  epic's team (RHOAI Docs) is an assumption — confirm with the strategy author.\n112\t  Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs, not from strategy-level\n113\t  ACs.\n114\t- **Availability preconditions, not ACs.** Both strategy open questions\n115\t  (hardware access, upstream image cadence) are externally controlled. They\n116\t  are recorded as risks on the affected epics; the ACs sit on engineering\n117\t  deliverables (the campaign report, the productized runtime, the validated\n118\t  training path), per the acceptance-criteria rules.\n119\t- **Docs priority exception.** E005 is P1 (strategy priority Major → P1)\n120\t  although HLR-4 is P2; the docs-authoring epic is exempt from priority\n121\t  inheritance.\n122\t- **Oversized investigation (E001), not split.** The validation campaign is a\n123\t  single logical investigation plausibly above the ~2-week guideline —\n124\t  estimated at 4–6 weeks of elapsed effort (securing hardware access, standing\n125\t  up vLLM ROCm serving on a recorded driver stack, running per-model\n126\t  functional and performance measurements, and writing the go/no-go report).\n127\t  It is not split because it is one investigation with one measurement plan,\n128\t  one hardware environment, and one go/no-go report: splitting it would\n129\t  duplicate campaign setup (environment, baseline methodology) and fragment\n130\t  the report the strategy gates on. Conditional re-split trigger: if the\n131\t  supported model set grows beyond roughly six models, or the campaign runs\n132\t  past two sprint cycles, split it along the measurement axis (functional\n133\t  validation vs. performance measurement vs. reporting) into separate\n134\t  Investigation epics sharing the recorded campaign environment.\n135\t- **Priority inversions.** None found: the P0 campaign gates the P0\n136\t  productization; training and quota work are P1 as their HLR dictates.\n137\t\n138\t## Tiered Delivery\n139\t\n140\tThe external dependency (upstream ROCm image cadence, and AMD hardware\n141\tavailability) has uncertain timing, but the strategy delivers partial value\n142\twithout release-aligned productization:\n143\t\n144\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E001 validation campaign (runs\n145\t  directly on upstream-published ROCm images), E003 PyTorch ROCm training\n146\t  enablement, E004 Kueue amd.com/gpu quota handling. Partial value:\n147\t  first-hand parity data, a supported training path, and quota readiness.\n148\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E002 productization — gated by the campaign's\n149\t  go/no-go outcome and subject to upstream ROCm image releases aligning with\n150\t  the platform's release trains.\n151\t- E005 (documentation) follows all implementation work per Rule 11.\n152\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd0bb8b882efc9cf","agent_id":"a8f1c7235544b55fc","session_id":"0cf99ff6-f962-401b-a34e-cfcb3645e2fd","uuid":"c1137d9b-08e2-4a28-bd36-e35b8505ba4e","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:42:57.634Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-93e92d